{
  "requirement": "`selector` addresses the item and `kind` describes it. `span` is a half-open byte range in the retained source. `structural_hash` identifies the item’s outline-relevant contents.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct OutlineItem {\n    pub selector: String,\n    pub kind: String,\n    pub span: Range<usize>,\n    pub structural_hash: String,\n}\n\nimpl OutlineItem {\n    pub fn new(\n        selector: String,\n        kind: String,\n        span: Range<usize>,\n        structural_hash: String,\n    ) -> Self;\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for OutlineItem;\nimpl Debug for OutlineItem;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "`requirements.sdk.outlines.OutlineItem` is an API-shape requirement. It specifies a `#[non_exhaustive]` struct with four public fields, the `new` constructor signature, and `Clone` and `Debug` implementations.\n\nThe prose says `selector` addresses the item, `kind` describes it, `span` is a half-open byte range, and `structural_hash` identifies the outline-relevant contents. I read that as descriptive of the fields. It adds no separate checkable behavior to this type, and `requirements.sdk.outlines.items` covers the outline semantics.\n\nThe requirement's `:test:` anchor and the `{test}` reference both point to `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]`.\n\nThat test generates rustdoc for the crate and walks every exported item. For `struct OutlineItem`, `documented_exports` calls `check_declaration`. `check_declaration` reads the requirement's own text from `md/requirements/sdk/outlines.md` and compares it with rustdoc output. The inventory entry in `md/requirements/sdk/public-api.md` links `struct OutlineItem` to this requirement's declaration.\n\nThe comparison enforces these obligations:\n- **Trait implementations:** the `impl Clone for OutlineItem;` and `impl Debug for OutlineItem;` block must match `rustdoc.trait_impls(page)` exactly. Dropping either derive, or adding an extra implementation, fails the test.\n- **Struct declaration and fields:** `signature_parts` normalizes the declared struct text and diffs it in both directions against rustdoc's declaration. A missing, extra, retyped or renamed field, a field that is not `pub`, or a missing `#[non_exhaustive]` attribute all fail.\n- **Constructor:** the `impl OutlineItem { pub fn new(...) -> Self; }` signature is diffed the same way, so a changed parameter order or type, or a changed return type, fails.\n- **`non_exhaustive`:** `check_declaration` has an explicit assertion that a struct with public fields is `#[non_exhaustive]`.\n- **Members:** `member_names` is compared against the declared member set, so extra public fields or methods are caught.\n\n`member_order_is_free_but_field_and_method_types_are_committed` also checks that the comparison helper detects changed field and method types.\n\nI found no gap. Any plausible violation of the declared API, such as a wrong field type, a missing derive, a changed `new` signature or a missing `non_exhaustive`, would make this test fail. The semantic descriptions of the fields add no separate behavior to test here.",
  "references": "34070f30c190da855a0af0db8758db88cea74ad388475c8cd2f1dfaa1fb9eb00",
